<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
Stage 2/3: MuJoCo ros2_control hardware block for the Franka Panda arm.

Clone of moveit_resources_panda_moveit_config's panda.ros2_control.xacro (same
macro name/params, so it's a drop-in swap in a top-level xacro), adding a
'mujoco' ros2_control_hardware_type branch alongside the existing
'mock_components' one. The 'isaac' branch from the original is intentionally
NOT carried over: Isaac Sim is no longer in the Stage 2/3 path (see
simulation/isaac_franka_moveit_bridge.py, kept only as deprecated reference).

Unlike the 'isaac' branch (topic_based_ros2_control), which let
pinn_controller_node bypass ros2_control's interface-claiming model entirely
and write straight to a topic, mujoco_ros2_control is a proper ros2_control
hardware plugin: both 'position' (for MoveIt2's default panda_arm_controller
execution) and 'effort' (for the Stage 3 computed-torque controller) command
interfaces are declared per joint, both backed by the SAME single motor
(direct-torque) actuator per joint in
mujoco/franka_emika_panda/panda_arm_mujoco.xml. mujoco_ros2_control binds
exactly one MuJoCo actuator per joint, so a separate position-servo actuator
alongside the motor actuator (tried first) doesn't work; see that MJCF file's
<actuator> comment. 'effort' writes torque straight to the actuator; 'position'
goes through mujoco_ros2_control's own internal PID (the pids_config_file
param below, config/mujoco_pid.yaml), which converts a position target into
torque on that same actuator.

The 'mujoco_model' hardware param points at that static, vendored MJCF file
rather than generating one at launch time from this URDF via
mujoco_ros2_control's robot_description_to_mjcf.sh: that tool has a
reproducible bug where the last link in a converted joint chain always gets
mis-placed and crashes ros2_control_node natively (confirmed across 3
different terminal links, unrelated to what that link actually contains), so
runtime conversion is not usable for this model. The vendored MJCF (from
Google DeepMind's mujoco_menagerie, see that file's own header comment) means
this repo now carries two Franka descriptions: this URDF for
robot_state_publisher/MoveIt2/RViz, and the MJCF for MuJoCo's physics, which
is the normal ros2_control pattern for any simulator (the same duality
Gazebo's URDF+SDF has), not a workaround.
-->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

    <xacro:macro name="panda_ros2_control" params="name initial_positions_file ros2_control_hardware_type arm_control_mode:=both">
        <xacro:property name="initial_positions" value="${xacro.load_yaml(initial_positions_file)['initial_positions']}"/>

        <!--
        arm_control_mode selects which command interface(s) the arm joints export:
          - 'both' (default): declares BOTH 'position' and 'effort' command
            interfaces + loads mujoco_pid.yaml, so MoveIt2's default position-mode
            panda_arm_controller (Milestone 1) and the Stage 3 effort-mode path
            (Milestone 2) can share the same launch, one at a time, via the usual
            switch_controller dance (ros2_ws/switch_to_effort.sh).
          - 'effort': declares ONLY the 'effort' command interface and omits the
            pids_config_file. In this mode MoveIt2's default position-mode
            panda_arm_controller CANNOT run (no position command interface for it
            to claim) -- use this only when Milestone 1's path isn't needed.

        2026-07-21/22 correction: an earlier version of this comment claimed that
        in 'both' mode mujoco_ros2_control permanently locks each motor actuator
        into position-PID control at registration time, silently discarding all
        effort commands (logged as a fixed per-joint "position control enabled
        (velocity, effort disabled)" state immune to controller-manager switching)
        -- and blamed this for a session where Stage 3 effort commands produced
        substantial, correctly time-varying torque but the arm stayed bit-for-bit
        frozen in position. That claim was written before the diagnostic it
        depended on (grepping ros2_control_node's startup log for per-joint
        "control enabled" messages) was actually run. When run, the log proved the
        opposite: perform_command_mode_switch() in mujoco_system_interface.cpp
        (github.com/ros-controls/mujoco_ros2_control) genuinely and correctly
        switches each joint from "position control enabled" to "effort control
        enabled" at runtime on controller switch, in 'both' mode, exactly as its
        source implements -- confirmed by both the live log (a single switch
        event, followed by "effort control enabled" persisting unchanged across a
        reset_world call and a full unload of panda_arm_controller, for the entire
        remainder of that session) and by reading write()'s actual ctrl-writing
        logic (correctly copies the effort command through when
        is_effort_control_enabled is true). The original frozen-arm bug's root
        cause was therefore NOT this. A clean relaunch in 'both' mode (this
        macro's default, unchanged) was subsequently confirmed via numeric
        /joint_states capture to produce real, substantial, multi-joint motion
        under Stage 3 effort control, so 'both' mode is not itself the problem.
        The 'effort' mode below was written as a candidate fix for the
        mislabeled cause and was never actually needed to resolve the freeze; it
        is kept only as a genuinely lower-complexity alternative for Stage-3-only
        testing where Milestone 1 coexistence isn't required.
        -->
        <ros2_control name="${name}" type="system">
            <hardware>
                <xacro:if value="${ros2_control_hardware_type == 'mock_components'}">
                    <plugin>mock_components/GenericSystem</plugin>
                </xacro:if>
                <xacro:if value="${ros2_control_hardware_type == 'mujoco'}">
                    <plugin>mujoco_ros2_control/MujocoSystemInterface</plugin>
                    <param name="mujoco_model">$(find pinn_franka_controller)/mujoco/franka_emika_panda/panda_arm_mujoco.xml</param>
                    <xacro:unless value="${arm_control_mode == 'effort'}">
                        <param name="pids_config_file">$(find pinn_franka_controller)/config/mujoco_pid.yaml</param>
                    </xacro:unless>
                    <param name="sim_speed_factor">1.0</param>
                    <param name="headless">$(arg headless)</param>
                </xacro:if>
            </hardware>
            <xacro:macro name="panda_arm_joint" params="joint_name">
                <joint name="${joint_name}">
                    <xacro:unless value="${arm_control_mode == 'effort'}">
                        <command_interface name="position"/>
                    </xacro:unless>
                    <command_interface name="effort"/>
                    <state_interface name="position">
                      <param name="initial_value">${initial_positions[joint_name]}</param>
                    </state_interface>
                    <state_interface name="velocity">
                      <param name="initial_value">0.0</param>
                    </state_interface>
                    <state_interface name="effort"/>
                </joint>
            </xacro:macro>
            <xacro:panda_arm_joint joint_name="panda_joint1"/>
            <xacro:panda_arm_joint joint_name="panda_joint2"/>
            <xacro:panda_arm_joint joint_name="panda_joint3"/>
            <xacro:panda_arm_joint joint_name="panda_joint4"/>
            <xacro:panda_arm_joint joint_name="panda_joint5"/>
            <xacro:panda_arm_joint joint_name="panda_joint6"/>
            <xacro:panda_arm_joint joint_name="panda_joint7"/>
        </ros2_control>
    </xacro:macro>
</robot>
